From d37ae6a3ad04bfe4c2e6fd76ed1940478251f6d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shaun Adams <s.adams@f5.com>
Date: Fri, 12 Jun 2026 10:47:12 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 12/14] transports: TenantScopedAccount + MultiTenantRouter +
 ctx tenant_id lift
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Foundational pieces of the per-tenant runtime dispatch — all
self-contained and tested, but not wired into the request path yet.
The inference handler refactor + server.go wiring lands in the next
patch.

Adds:

  lib.TenantScopedAccount — schemas.Account implementation that holds
    an immutable per-tenant snapshot of providers (the map returned by
    configstore.GetProvidersConfigByTenant from patch 0015). The
    MultiTenantBifrost loader will hand one of these to bifrost.Init
    so each tenant runtime only ever sees its own keys.

  lib.MultiTenantRouter — BifrostRouter implementation that dispatches
    via multitenant.Manager. Resolution order:
      1. ctx.Value(BifrostContextKeyTenantID) -> Manager.Acquire(tid)
      2. FallbackTenant set                   -> Manager.Acquire(fallback)
      3. FallbackClient set                   -> serve it, noop release
      4. otherwise                            -> error

    The fallback options together cover the transition window between
    this patch and every entry point being routed through the resolver
    — single-tenant smoke deployments can wire FallbackClient to the
    process-global *bifrost.Bifrost without forcing every request
    through a Manager acquire. Production multi-tenant deployments
    leave both empty and require the resolver to set a tenant id.

  lib/ctx.go: tenant id is lifted from the fasthttp RequestCtx
    UserValue (where TenantResolverMiddleware writes it) onto the
    BifrostContext under the typed multitenant.BifrostContextKeyTenantID
    key. The middleware writes under the string form because fasthttp
    UserValue requires it; the explicit lift makes the typed-key
    reader path canonical so downstream code doesn't need to know
    about the fallback.

Tests:
  - account_test.go: 5 TestTenantScopedAccount_* covering empty map,
    multi-provider listing, key filtering via the IncludeOnlyKeys
    context value (matching BaseAccount semantics), and the
    NetworkConfig / ConcurrencyAndBufferSize default-fallback path.
  - multitenant_router_test.go: 8 TestMultiTenantRouter_* covering
    nil-Manager panic, ctx-typed-key happy path, string-form-key
    fallback, FallbackTenant via Manager, FallbackClient bypass, the
    no-tenant-no-fallback error, loader-error wrap, and the Manager
    accessor.

Full framework/configstore + plugins/governance + multitenant +
transports/bifrost-http/{handlers,lib} test suites pass.

Ref: https://jira.f5net.com/browse/XC-25496
